/*
  Steps that arrived as flattened table text in more than one plan, cleaned once for every plan
  that carries them. A plan-specific entry above wins over these.
*/
export const GENERAL_STEP_FIXES = new Map([
  ["AgentMaximisesA trade-off it faces HouseholdsUTILITY (satisfaction): they earn income to buy goods and servicesA household will not take an expensive luxury holiday if it means living in poverty for the rest of the year FirmsPROFIT (total revenue minus total cost), which can be reinvested to raise quality or cut costs, or paid out to ownersNew equipment has an upfront cost but makes production cheaper later GovernmentWELFARE: growth, lower inequality and unemployment, protecting the environment.", "Households maximise utility (satisfaction) and earn income to buy goods and services, and they face a trade-off, since a household will not take an expensive luxury holiday if it means living in poverty for the rest of the year. Firms maximise profit (total revenue minus total cost), which can be reinvested to raise quality or cut costs, or paid out to owners, and their trade-off is that new equipment has an upfront cost but makes production cheaper later. Governments maximise welfare, meaning growth, lower inequality and unemployment, and protecting the environment."],
  ["It shapes resource allocation through state provision, subsidies and bansMoney spent in one area is not available elsewhere", "It shapes resource allocation through state provision, subsidies and bans. Money spent in one area is not available elsewhere."],
  ["FeatureExplanation High barriers to entryEconomies of scale, brand loyalty, sunk costs, legal barriers InterdependenceEach firm’s best action depends on what rivals do Price rigidityPrices tend to be stable; competition happens on non-price terms Non-price competitionAdvertising, branding, loyalty schemes, quality, product development", "The features are high barriers to entry, such as economies of scale, brand loyalty, sunk costs and legal barriers. Firms are interdependent, because each firm's best action depends on what rivals do. Prices tend to be stable (price rigidity), so competition happens on non-price terms such as advertising, branding, loyalty schemes, quality and product development."],
  ["Type of goodYED value and meaningExamples Normal good (by sign)YED > 0: demand rises as income risesClothes, restaurant meals, home improvement Inferior good (by sign)YED < 0: demand falls as income risesBus journeys Luxury good, income elastic (by size)YED > 1: demand changes proportionally MORE than incomeForeign travel, expensive handbags Necessity good, income inelastic (by size)0 < YED < 1: demand changes proportionally LESS than incomeFood, toilet roll, deodorant A good can be both a normal good and a luxury. \"Normal\" only means demand rises with income; \"luxury\" adds that it rises by MORE than income does.", "Normal goods (by sign) have YED above 0, so demand rises as income rises, for example clothes, restaurant meals and home improvement, while inferior goods (by sign) have YED below 0, so demand falls as income rises, for example bus journeys. Luxury goods are income elastic (by size) with YED above 1, so demand changes proportionally more than income, for example foreign travel and expensive handbags, while necessity goods are income inelastic (by size) with YED between 0 and 1, so demand changes proportionally less than income, for example food, toilet roll and deodorant. A good can be both a normal good and a luxury, because \"normal\" only means demand rises with income, while \"luxury\" adds that it rises by more than income does."],
  ["UseExplanation Total revenueIt tells a firm how to maximise revenue through price: on the ELASTIC part of the demand curve cut the price, on the INELASTIC part raise it Volatility of supplySome markets have large swings in supply (harvests, for example).", "Total revenue: it tells a firm how to maximise revenue through price, so on the elastic part of the demand curve it should cut the price and on the inelastic part it should raise it. Volatility of supply: some markets have large swings in supply (harvests, for example)."],
  ["Cigarette demand is inelastic, so tax rises are largely passed on to smokers Sales and discountsIt shows whether a promotion will raise quantity enough to raise revenue Price discriminationGroups with more inelastic demand can be charged higher prices to raise revenue, for example train commuters Trade unionsIf a union knows demand for the industry's product is inelastic, it can push harder for higher wages, because firms can pass the cost on in prices without losing much quantity, and so without cutting back on labour", "Cigarette demand is inelastic, so tax rises are largely passed on to smokers, and for sales and discounts it shows whether a promotion will raise quantity enough to raise revenue. For price discrimination, groups with more inelastic demand can be charged higher prices to raise revenue, for example train commuters. For trade unions, if a union knows demand for the industry's product is inelastic, it can push harder for higher wages, because firms can pass the cost on in prices without losing much quantity, and so without cutting back on labour."],
  ["UseExplanation Forecasting future demandIf incomes are forecast to rise and the firm knows its YED, it knows how much quantity demanded will rise or fall.", "Forecasting future demand: if incomes are forecast to rise and the firm knows its YED, it knows how much quantity demanded will rise or fall."],
  ["XED and relationshipWhat makes the value largeExample Positive: substitutesHow close the substitutes areSamsung and Apple phones would have a higher positive XED than Apple and Nokia phones, so they are more strongly related Negative: complementsHow strongly the goods go togetherToothpaste and mouthwash would have a larger absolute negative XED than cakes and whipped cream, because cakes have many other toppings Zero: unrelatedThere is no link to measureTVs and economics textbooks The SIZE of the XED shows the strength of the link between a price change in one good and the change in demand for the other.", "A positive XED means the goods are substitutes, and the value is larger the closer the substitutes are, so Samsung and Apple phones would have a higher positive XED than Apple and Nokia phones and are more strongly related. A negative XED means the goods are complements, and the absolute value is larger the more strongly the goods go together, so toothpaste and mouthwash would have a larger absolute negative XED than cakes and whipped cream, because cakes have many other toppings. A zero XED means the goods are unrelated because there is no link to measure, for example TVs and economics textbooks, and the size of the XED shows the strength of the link between a price change in one good and the change in demand for the other."],
  ["MeasureWhat it capturesLimitation Real GDP per capita (PPP)Average material living standardsIgnores distribution, informal economy, externalities and non-material welfare HDIIncome, life expectancy and years of schooling combinedOnly three dimensions; ignores inequality, gender and the environment Inequality-adjusted HDIHDI discounted for how unequally its components are distributedData-hungry and unavailable for many countries Multidimensional Poverty IndexDeprivation across health, education and living standardsComplex; thresholds are somewhat arbitrary Other indicatorsLiteracy, infant mortality, access to clean water, mobile subscriptionsEach captures one dimension only", "Real GDP per capita (PPP) captures average material living standards but ignores distribution, the informal economy, externalities and non-material welfare, while HDI combines income, life expectancy and years of schooling but covers only three dimensions and ignores inequality, gender and the environment. Inequality-adjusted HDI discounts HDI for how unequally its components are distributed but is data-hungry and unavailable for many countries, and the Multidimensional Poverty Index measures deprivation across health, education and living standards but is complex and its thresholds are somewhat arbitrary. Other indicators such as literacy, infant mortality, access to clean water and mobile subscriptions each capture one dimension only."],
  ["MeasureWhat it means Real GDPGDP adjusted for inflation, so it measures volume rather than price changes.", "Real GDP is GDP adjusted for inflation, so it measures volume rather than price changes."],
  ["GDP per capitaReal GDP ÷ population.", "GDP per capita is real GDP divided by population."],
  ["GNIGross National Income - includes income earned abroad by residents and excludes income earned domestically by foreigners.", "GNI, or Gross National Income, includes income earned abroad by residents and excludes income earned domestically by foreigners."],
  ["MeasureMethodAdvantageDisadvantage Labour Force SurveyONS questionnaire of about 60,000 households.", "The Labour Force Survey is an ONS questionnaire of about 60,000 households."],
  ["Counts anyone out of work who has actively sought work in the last 4 weeks and can start within 2 weeks.Uses the ILO definition, so international comparison is possible.", "It counts anyone out of work who has actively sought work in the last 4 weeks and can start within 2 weeks. It uses the ILO definition, so international comparison is possible."],
  ["Claimant countThe number of people claiming Jobseeker’s Allowance or the equivalent Universal Credit element each month.Cheap, fast and complete - the data already exists.Excludes anyone not eligible or not claiming, so it understates.", "The claimant count is the number of people claiming Jobseeker’s Allowance or the equivalent Universal Credit element each month. It is cheap, fast and complete because the data already exists. It excludes anyone not eligible or not claiming, so it understates."],
  ["Case and exampleWhat is counted and what is ignoredResult Negative externality of CONSUMPTION: smoking cigarettesCOUNTED (private benefits): stress relief, liking the taste.", "Case and example: a negative externality of consumption, smoking cigarettes. Counted (private benefits): stress relief, liking the taste."],
  ["TypeExampleEffect Negative CONSUMPTION externalitySmoking, alcohol, sugar - passive smoking, NHS costs, antisocial behaviourMSB below MPB; over-consumption at Qm > Q* Positive PRODUCTION externalityR&D and training - knowledge spillovers benefit rival firms and the wider economyMSC below MPC; under-production at Qm < Q* Identify PRODUCTION or CONSUMPTION first - that tells you whether the divergence is on the cost side or the benefit side - then POSITIVE or NEGATIVE, which tells you which curve lies above which.", "A negative consumption externality, such as smoking, alcohol and sugar, causes passive smoking, NHS costs and antisocial behaviour, so MSB is below MPB and there is over-consumption at Qm > Q*. A positive production externality, such as R&D and training, creates knowledge spillovers that benefit rival firms and the wider economy, so MSC is below MPC and there is under-production at Qm < Q*. Identify production or consumption first, which tells you whether the divergence is on the cost side or the benefit side, then positive or negative, which tells you which curve lies above which."],
  ["FactorExplanation The school of thoughtIn the NEO-CLASSICAL view a negative gap matters little: disequilibrium unemployment is quickly resolved as wages fall and SRAS shifts right, restoring full employment.", "School of thought: in the neo-classical view a negative gap matters little, as disequilibrium unemployment is quickly resolved when wages fall and SRAS shifts right, restoring full employment."],
  ["ProblemExplanation Adverse selectionThe informed party self-selects to the detriment of the uninformed.", "Adverse selection is when the informed party self-selects to the detriment of the uninformed."],
  ["Moral hazardOnce insured, a party takes more risk because they no longer bear the full cost.", "Moral hazard is when, once insured, a party takes more risk because they no longer bear the full cost."],
  ["Type of taxExplanationExamples DirectCharged on a person's or firm's income or profit and paid by them directly to the governmentIncome tax, National Insurance, corporation tax IndirectCharged on goods and services, collected by the seller and passed to the governmentVAT, excise duties on fuel, alcohol and tobacco A tax on a good raises the cost of supplying it, which is the main non-price factor shifting supply, so the supply curve shifts LEFT.", "Direct taxes are charged on a person's or firm's income or profit and paid by them directly to the government, such as income tax, National Insurance and corporation tax. Indirect taxes are charged on goods and services, collected by the seller and passed to the government, such as VAT and excise duties on fuel, alcohol and tobacco. A tax on a good raises the cost of supplying it, which is the main non-price factor shifting supply, so the supply curve shifts LEFT."],
  ["MethodHow it worksProblem RPI − X price cappingPrices may rise by inflation minus an efficiency factor X, forcing real price falls and passing efficiency gains to consumersSetting X requires knowing the firm’s efficiency potential, which only the firm knows.", "RPI − X price capping: prices may rise by inflation minus an efficiency factor X, forcing real price falls and passing efficiency gains to consumers. The problem is that setting X requires knowing the firm’s efficiency potential, which only the firm knows."],
  ["Too tight and it cannot invest; too loose and consumers gain nothing Rate of return regulationCaps profit as a percentage of capital employedCreates a perverse incentive to over-invest in capital to raise the allowed profit Quality standardsMinimum service levels enforced by the regulatorCostly to monitor; firms meet the letter and not the spirit Yardstick competitionCompare regional monopolies against each other and penalise laggardsOnly works where genuinely comparable firms exist", "Too tight and it cannot invest, while too loose and consumers gain nothing. Rate of return regulation caps profit as a percentage of capital employed, which creates a perverse incentive to over-invest in capital to raise the allowed profit, while quality standards set minimum service levels enforced by the regulator but are costly to monitor, as firms meet the letter and not the spirit. Yardstick competition compares regional monopolies against each other and penalises laggards, but it only works where genuinely comparable firms exist."],
  ["ToolHow it works Promotion of small businessGrants, start-up loans and simplified regulation for small firms lower the barriers that protect incumbents, making markets more contestable from below DeregulationRemoving licensing and entry rules that exist mainly to protect established firms, so new entrants can compete on merit Competitive tenderingGovernment contracts are awarded through open bidding rather than to an incumbent automatically, forcing even a monopoly supplier to bid competitively for that portion of demand Competitive tendering is easy to confuse with privatisation.", "Promotion of small business uses grants, start-up loans and simplified regulation for small firms to lower the barriers that protect incumbents, making markets more contestable from below. Deregulation removes licensing and entry rules that exist mainly to protect established firms, so new entrants can compete on merit. Competitive tendering awards government contracts through open bidding rather than to an incumbent automatically, forcing even a monopoly supplier to bid competitively for that portion of demand, and it is easy to confuse with privatisation."],
  ["ProblemExplanation The same goods are not compared each yearProducts improve in quality over time, so a price rise may reflect a better product rather than a more expensive one.", "The same goods are not compared each year, because products improve in quality over time, so a price rise may reflect a better product rather than a more expensive one."],
  ["Inflation tends to be overstatedWeights are updated only annually, so the index does not capture households substituting away from goods whose prices rise during the year.", "Inflation tends to be overstated. Weights are updated only annually, so the index does not capture households substituting away from goods whose prices rise during the year."],
  ["There is no \"typical\" householdPensioners, students and families have very different baskets, so the headline rate matches almost nobody’s actual experience.", "There is no \"typical\" household. Pensioners, students and families have very different baskets, so the headline rate matches almost nobody’s actual experience."],
  ["StepWhat happens 1.", ""],
  ["WeightingEach item is weighted by its share of typical household spending, taken from the Living Costs and Food Survey.", "Weighting means each item is weighted by its share of typical household spending, taken from the Living Costs and Food Survey."],
  ["Type of targetWhat it meansExample SymmetricPolicy-makers must act if inflation falls BELOW target as well as if it rises above itThe Bank of England's 2% target, with a band of one percentage point either side AsymmetricAction is required only if inflation rises above the targetThe European Central Bank defined price stability as inflation \"below, but close to, 2%\" until it adopted a symmetric 2% target in 2021 A symmetric target is better for the UK because with an asymmetric one policy-makers are tempted to UNDERSHOOT the target, risking deflation and weak growth.", "A symmetric target means policy-makers must act if inflation falls below the target as well as if it rises above it, such as the Bank of England's 2% target with a band of one percentage point either side. An asymmetric target means action is required only if inflation rises above the target, as with the European Central Bank, which defined price stability as inflation \"below, but close to, 2%\" until it adopted a symmetric 2% target in 2021. A symmetric target is better for the UK because with an asymmetric one policy-makers are tempted to undershoot the target, risking deflation and weak growth."],
  ["CostExplanation Shoe leather costsHolding cash loses value, so households and firms move money more often.", "Shoe leather costs arise because holding cash loses value, so households and firms move money more often."],
  ["Menu costsFirms must update prices more often - menus, catalogues, labels.", "Menu costs arise because firms must update prices more often, such as on menus, catalogues and labels."],
  ["Unequal redistribution of incomeLosers: those with weak wage bargaining power, people on fixed incomes and benefits, net savers (the real value of savings falls) and creditors (the real value of repayments falls).", "Inflation redistributes income unequally. The losers are those with weak wage bargaining power, people on fixed incomes and benefits, net savers (the real value of savings falls) and creditors (the real value of repayments falls)."],
  ["It depends onWhy What is rising in priceRising food and energy prices hit low-income households hardest because those are necessities with inelastic demand.", "How serious inflation is depends on what is rising in price. Rising food and energy prices hit low-income households hardest because those are necessities with inelastic demand."],
  ["The causeCost-push is worse than demand-pull because it comes with falling real output.", "How serious inflation is also depends on the cause. Cost-push inflation is worse than demand-pull because it comes with falling real output."],
  ["VolatilityFluctuation creates uncertainty and deters investment even at a low average rate.", "Volatility matters because fluctuation in inflation creates uncertainty and deters investment even at a low average rate."],
  ["BenefitExplanation Real wage flexibilityWages are sticky downwards - workers resist nominal cuts.", "Real wage flexibility is a benefit, because wages are sticky downwards and workers resist nominal cuts."],
  ["TypeDefinitionExample DirectLevied on income or wealth; cannot be passed onIncome tax, corporation tax, national insurance, inheritance tax IndirectLevied on spending; the burden can be passed to consumersVAT, fuel duty, alcohol and tobacco duty ProgressiveThe average rate RISES as income risesUK income tax - 20%, 40%, 45% bands ProportionalThe average rate stays constantA flat tax RegressiveThe average rate FALLS as income risesVAT, because poorer households spend a larger share of income", "Direct taxes are levied on income or wealth and cannot be passed on, such as income tax, corporation tax, national insurance and inheritance tax, while indirect taxes are levied on spending and the burden can be passed to consumers, such as VAT, fuel duty and alcohol and tobacco duty. A progressive tax has an average rate that RISES as income rises, such as UK income tax with its 20%, 40% and 45% bands, and a proportional tax keeps the average rate constant, such as a flat tax. A regressive tax has an average rate that FALLS as income rises, such as VAT, because poorer households spend a larger share of income."],
  ["Definition: Private goodExcludable and rival - the normal case, which the market supplies efficiently.", "A private good is excludable and rival, the normal case, which the market supplies efficiently."],
  ["Feature of a public goodMeaningExample Non-excludablePeople cannot be excluded from consuming the good even if they have not paidA lamppost: you cannot be stopped from using its light, and it would be impractical to charge everyone who walks past Non-rival (non-diminishable)One person's consumption does not affect anyone else'sDefence: protecting one citizen does not stop another being protected too Non-rejectableBecause the supply is collective, people cannot choose not to consume itA nuclear defence system: individuals cannot opt out A PURE public good has all of these features.", "A public good is non-excludable, meaning people cannot be excluded from consuming it even if they have not paid, such as a lamppost, where you cannot be stopped from using its light and it would be impractical to charge everyone who walks past. It is non-rival (non-diminishable), meaning one person's consumption does not affect anyone else's, such as defence, where protecting one citizen does not stop another being protected too. It is also non-rejectable, because the supply is collective and people cannot choose not to consume it, such as a nuclear defence system where individuals cannot opt out, and a PURE public good has all of these features."],
  ["MeasureEffectDrawback Raise taxesIncreases revenue directlyReduces AD and may reduce incentives; politically costly Cut spendingReduces expenditure directlyNegative multiplier deepens a downturn; falls on public services Promote growthRaises revenue and cuts benefit spending automaticallySlow, and may require spending first Raise the retirement ageCuts pension spending and raises the labour forcePolitically difficult; regressive since life expectancy varies by income Sell assetsOne-off revenueLoses the future income stream permanently", "Raising taxes increases revenue directly but reduces AD, may reduce incentives and is politically costly, while cutting spending reduces expenditure directly but the negative multiplier deepens a downturn and the cuts fall on public services. Promoting growth raises revenue and cuts benefit spending automatically but is slow and may require spending first, while raising the retirement age cuts pension spending and raises the labour force but is politically difficult and regressive since life expectancy varies by income. Selling assets gives one-off revenue but loses the future income stream permanently."],
  ["ObjectiveWhat the data showThe explanation Economic growthReal GDP has grown over time, since most annual figures are positive.", "Economic growth: real GDP has grown over time, since most annual figures are positive."],
  ["The two recent periods of NEGATIVE growth are the financial crisis (from 2007) and the coronavirus pandemic (2020)Both were large negative shocks to demand and supply: a banking collapse in one case and the lockdown of whole sectors in the other UnemploymentBefore the pandemic unemployment was historically low, at around 4%.", "The two recent periods of NEGATIVE growth are the financial crisis (from 2007) and the coronavirus pandemic (2020). Both were large negative shocks to demand and supply, a banking collapse in one case and the lockdown of whole sectors in the other. Before the pandemic, unemployment was historically low, at around 4%."],
  ["EffectMechanismWhen it dominates Crowding OUTGovernment borrowing raises demand for loanable funds, raising interest rates and displacing private investmentAt full capacity, with rates well above zero Resource crowding outThe state competes for scarce workers and materials, bidding up their priceWhen the economy is at or near full employment Crowding INPublic infrastructure raises the expected return on private investment, so private investment RISESWith spare capacity, low rates, and high-return public projects Crowding out is the standard evaluation of any fiscal expansion, but state the CONDITION.", "Crowding OUT happens when government borrowing raises demand for loanable funds, raising interest rates and displacing private investment, which dominates at full capacity with rates well above zero. Resource crowding out is when the state competes for scarce workers and materials, bidding up their price, which dominates when the economy is at or near full employment. Crowding IN happens when public infrastructure raises the expected return on private investment, so private investment RISES, which dominates with spare capacity, low rates and high-return public projects. Crowding out is the standard evaluation of any fiscal expansion, but state the CONDITION."],
  ["Cause of deflationBeneficial?Why A fall in ADNOReal GDP falls.", "Deflation caused by a fall in AD is not beneficial, because real GDP falls."],
  ["AdvantageExplanation CompetitionLittle red tape means more firms compete for consumers.", "One advantage of market economies is competition, because little red tape means more firms compete for consumers."],
  ["That pushes prices down as firms minimise costs to gain an edge, and encourages them to expand output and gain economies of scale Quality and innovationFirms invest in R&D to improve products and create new ones, raising consumer satisfaction and market share in the search for profit.", "That pushes prices down as firms minimise costs to gain an edge, and encourages them to expand output and gain economies of scale. Quality and innovation also benefit, as firms invest in R&D to improve products and create new ones, raising consumer satisfaction and market share in the search for profit."],
  ["Firms that fail to innovate lose out: Nokia fell far behind in mobile phones while Apple kept innovating ChoiceR&D leads to greater variety, so more people's needs are met and quality of life rises Allocative efficiencyThe price mechanism rations, incentivises and signals, so there is no lasting excess demand or excess supply Productive efficiencyProfit maximisation stops firms becoming slack and producing less efficiently than they could EntrepreneurshipThe chance to make profit encourages people to start businesses, which raises employment and economic growth ProductivityWorkers who know that working hard earns higher wages are motivated to gain new skills, so the economy becomes more productive over time Economic growthThe profit incentive and entrepreneurship matter a great deal in a globalised world where international competitiveness is vital, and overseas competition drives prices down Environmental standardsIt can even be argued that demand for greener products grows over time, so firms that supply them gain a comparative advantage DisadvantageExplanation Market failureAllocation is not always efficient (healthcare, education).", "Firms that fail to innovate lose out: Nokia fell far behind in mobile phones while Apple kept innovating. Market economies offer choice, as R&D leads to greater variety, so more people's needs are met and quality of life rises. They also give allocative efficiency, as the price mechanism rations, incentivises and signals, so there is no lasting excess demand or excess supply, and productive efficiency, as profit maximisation stops firms becoming slack and producing less efficiently than they could. Entrepreneurship also benefits, as the chance to make profit encourages people to start businesses, which raises employment and economic growth, and productivity improves, as workers who know that working hard earns higher wages are motivated to gain new skills, so the economy becomes more productive over time. The profit incentive and entrepreneurship matter a great deal for economic growth in a globalised world where international competitiveness is vital, and overseas competition drives prices down. On environmental standards, it can even be argued that demand for greener products grows over time, so firms that supply them gain a comparative advantage. A disadvantage is market failure, as allocation is not always efficient (healthcare, education)."],
  ["The main aim is profit, so third-party costs and benefits are not always counted, especially long-term harms such as environmental damage or smoking Income inequalityInequality tends to be larger in market-based economies (such as the US and India), especially where there is little social protection through benefits and healthcare.", "The main aim is profit, so third-party costs and benefits are not always counted, especially long-term harms such as environmental damage or smoking. Income inequality tends to be larger in market-based economies (such as the US and India), especially where there is little social protection through benefits and healthcare."],
  ["There is an incentive to work harder and gain skills, but inequality of opportunity limits it: not everyone can get a good education, the poor may have to go straight into work (a higher opportunity cost of studying) while the wealthy can afford to acquire skills Concentrated competitionMarkets can come to be dominated by one or a few firms, perhaps through brand loyalty, so consumers are more likely to be exploited, for example through higher prices Public goodsA free market does not provide them at all because of the free rider problem", "There is an incentive to work harder and gain skills, but inequality of opportunity limits it: not everyone can get a good education, the poor may have to go straight into work (a higher opportunity cost of studying) while the wealthy can afford to acquire skills. Concentrated competition means markets can come to be dominated by one or a few firms, perhaps through brand loyalty, so consumers are more likely to be exploited, for example through higher prices. A free market does not provide public goods at all because of the free rider problem."],
  ["DrawbackExplanation CostHS2 was first estimated at £32.7bn and later estimates exceeded £100bn.", "Cost is a drawback, as HS2 was first estimated at £32.7bn and later estimates exceeded £100bn."],
  ["A third runway at Heathrow was predicted to cost £49bn Negative externalitiesLarge projects impose costs on people nearby: living next to a nuclear power station or a busy motorway Higher unemployment in placesImmigrants may work for less than local workers, and send remittances home, which reduces the size of the multiplier.", "A third runway at Heathrow was predicted to cost £49bn. Large projects can also create negative externalities by imposing costs on people nearby, such as living next to a nuclear power station or a busy motorway. Immigrants may work for less than local workers, which can mean higher unemployment in some places, and they send remittances home, which reduces the size of the multiplier."],
  ["Investment in capital may substitute for labour Effectiveness depends onWhy The state of the economyRaising productive capacity does nothing if the economy is on the Keynesian (horizontal) part of AS.", "Investment in capital may substitute for labour. The effectiveness of supply-side policy also depends on the state of the economy, because raising productive capacity does nothing if the economy is on the Keynesian (horizontal) part of AS."],
  ["Cutting benefits in a recession may push people to look for work that does not exist QualityEducation spending that does not produce the skills the economy needs fails, which is why there is more emphasis on STEM subjects.", "Cutting benefits in a recession may push people to look for work that does not exist. Quality also matters, because education spending that does not produce the skills the economy needs fails, which is why there is more emphasis on STEM subjects."],
  ["TypeExplanation Risk-bearingA larger firm can diversify products and markets, so a downturn in one does not threaten the whole business.", "Risk-bearing economies of scale arise because a larger firm can diversify products and markets, so a downturn in one does not threaten the whole business."],
  ["TechnicalLarge-scale capital - a production line, a bigger ship - spreads its cost over more units.", "Technical economies of scale arise because large-scale capital, such as a production line or a bigger ship, spreads its cost over more units."],
  ["FinancialBanks lend to large firms at lower interest rates because they are lower risk and have more collateral.", "Financial economies of scale arise because banks lend to large firms at lower interest rates, as they are lower risk and have more collateral."],
  ["ManagerialSpecialist managers can be employed - a dedicated finance director, HR director - raising productivity through the division of labour.", "Managerial economies of scale arise because specialist managers can be employed, such as a dedicated finance director or HR director, raising productivity through the division of labour."],
  ["FactorWhy it changes the answer Nature and size of the industryWhere fixed costs are huge - pharmaceuticals, car manufacturing - exploiting scale is essential.", "The nature and size of the industry changes the answer. Where fixed costs are huge, as in pharmaceuticals and car manufacturing, exploiting scale is essential."],
  ["For a small local business it barely matters Price elasticity of demandA firm facing inelastic demand may make MORE profit by restricting output and holding price high, rather than expanding to chase lower unit costs Position relative to the MESA firm already at or near minimum efficient scale should be wary of expanding, because the next step may bring diseconomies instead Limits on global advertisingLanguage and cultural barriers force adaptation, and targeted digital advertising has cut per-unit costs for small firms anyway - so marketing economies are weaker than they were Diseconomies of scaleCo-ordination and communication problems can outweigh the technical savings as output rises Monopoly supplierPurchasing economies fail if there is only one place to buy the input - bulk buying gives no leverage against a sole supplier X-inefficiency (slack)A firm can have every economy of scale available to it and still run at high average cost, because nothing forces it to control that cost", "For a small local business it barely matters. Economies of scale matter less when demand is price inelastic, because a firm may make more profit by restricting output and holding price high rather than expanding to chase lower unit costs, and when a firm is already at or near minimum efficient scale, because it should be wary of expanding as the next step may bring diseconomies instead. They are also limited by global advertising barriers, as language and cultural barriers force adaptation and targeted digital advertising has cut per-unit costs for small firms anyway, so marketing economies are weaker than they were, by diseconomies of scale, as co-ordination and communication problems can outweigh the technical savings as output rises, by a monopoly supplier, as purchasing economies fail if there is only one place to buy the input and bulk buying gives no leverage against a sole supplier, and by X-inefficiency (slack), as a firm can have every economy of scale available to it and still run at high average cost because nothing forces it to control that cost."],
  ["Trade-offWhy it may not happen POLLUTION may not fall: switching to electric cars still needs large amounts of resources, and much of the electricity still comes from non-renewable sourcesGrowth gives the government more money to invest in green transport such as electric buses INFLATION: packaging changes, recycling costs and charges such as the ultra-low emission zone raise costs for firmsGreener machinery can be more efficient, electricity for electric vehicles is cheap, and firms can earn money by recycling materials INEQUALITY: replacing fossil fuels needs huge capital spending that may raise energy costs, which hit low-income households hardestIn the long run renewable energy should be cheaper and not exposed to volatile world oil and gas prices UNEMPLOYMENT: workers in polluting industries lose jobs if firms close because of pollution rulesMany jobs have been created in renewable energy, carbon capture research and recycling", "Pollution may not fall, because switching to electric cars still needs large amounts of resources and much of the electricity still comes from non-renewable sources, but growth gives the government more money to invest in green transport such as electric buses. Inflation may not rise, even though packaging changes, recycling costs and charges such as the ultra-low emission zone raise costs for firms, because greener machinery can be more efficient, electricity for electric vehicles is cheap, and firms can earn money by recycling materials. Inequality may not worsen, even though replacing fossil fuels needs huge capital spending that may raise energy costs, which hit low-income households hardest, because in the long run renewable energy should be cheaper and not exposed to volatile world oil and gas prices, and unemployment may not rise, even though workers in polluting industries lose jobs if firms close because of pollution rules, because many jobs have been created in renewable energy, carbon capture research and recycling."],
  ["Why pollution might fall as income risesWhy it might not Greater awareness and regulation: cleaner air is politically popular once basic needs are met (London's congestion charge and ultra-low emission zone came from residents' demand)Growth means more resource use: even a move into services brings more air travel and more cars Better technology uses fewer raw materials (electric cars, solar power)Rich countries can EXPORT their environmental damage by buying goods made elsewhere, or meat produced on deforested land Structural change: moving from the primary to the secondary sector raises pollution, but moving from secondary to tertiary lowers itPollution is not a simple function of income; the effectiveness of regulation and many other factors matter", "Pollution might fall as income rises because of greater awareness and regulation, as cleaner air is politically popular once basic needs are met (London's congestion charge and ultra-low emission zone came from residents' demand), because better technology uses fewer raw materials (electric cars, solar power), and because structural change from the secondary to the tertiary sector lowers pollution, although moving from the primary to the secondary sector raises it. It might not fall because growth means more resource use, as even a move into services brings more air travel and more cars, and because rich countries can export their environmental damage by buying goods made elsewhere, or meat produced on deforested land. Pollution is not a simple function of income, as the effectiveness of regulation and many other factors matter."],
  ["ConsequenceExplanation Withdrawal from the circular flowA rising deficit means (X − M) falls, so AD falls: output falls, derived demand for labour falls, unemployment and benefit spending rise, tax revenue falls, and the budget deficit widens.", "A rising deficit is a withdrawal from the circular flow. It means (X − M) falls, so AD falls, output falls, derived demand for labour falls, unemployment and benefit spending rise, tax revenue falls, and the budget deficit widens."],
  ["Structural unemploymentFalling exports and rising imports cause deindustrialisation, concentrated regionally.", "Structural unemployment can result, because falling exports and rising imports cause deindustrialisation, concentrated regionally."],
  ["Loss of policy freedomCountries in a currency union cannot devalue, so adjustment must come through wages and prices - the southern European experience between 2000 and 2007.", "There is a loss of policy freedom, because countries in a currency union cannot devalue, so adjustment must come through wages and prices, as in the southern European experience between 2000 and 2007."],
  ["It depends onWhy Its size relative to GDPA few percent of GDP is easily financed; a large deficit is not.", "It depends on its size relative to GDP, because a few percent of GDP is easily financed but a large deficit is not."],
  ["How it is financedLong-term FDI is stable and productive; short-term \"hot money\" can reverse overnight.", "It depends on how it is financed, because long-term FDI is stable and productive but short-term \"hot money\" can reverse overnight."],
  ["What the imports areCapital goods raise future capacity; consumer goods do not.", "Capital goods raise future capacity, while consumer goods do not."],
  ["Positive consequenceExplanation It brings capacity-raising investmentA current account deficit means a financial account SURPLUS, for example high inward direct investment.", "It brings capacity-raising investment, because a current account deficit means a financial account surplus, for example high inward direct investment."],
  ["Investment in infrastructure, such as Chinese-financed projects in Africa like a coastal railway in Nigeria, can raise incomes and tax revenue, which can be spent on health and education to improve development Automatic correctionA large deficit tends to cause the currency to depreciate under a floating rate, which makes exports cheaper and imports dearer and so reduces the deficit It may reflect high incomesRich consumers buy a greater variety of better goods at lower prices from around the world, and firms gain from cheaper inputs, which is not a cause for concern It may reflect demographicsPeople aged about 40 to 65 save for retirement and tend to run surpluses, while retirees run down savings and spend heavily on imports Whether a deficit is a concern depends onWhy Its size relative to GDPA deficit of over about 5% of GDP is more worrying because it relies heavily on foreigners continuing to invest.", "Investment in infrastructure, such as Chinese-financed projects in Africa like a coastal railway in Nigeria, can raise incomes and tax revenue, which can be spent on health and education to improve development. A large deficit tends to cause the currency to depreciate under a floating rate, which makes exports cheaper and imports dearer and so reduces the deficit. A deficit may also reflect high incomes, because rich consumers buy a greater variety of better goods at lower prices from around the world and firms gain from cheaper inputs, which is not a cause for concern, or demographics, because people aged about 40 to 65 save for retirement and tend to run surpluses while retirees run down savings and spend heavily on imports, and whether a deficit is a concern depends on its size relative to GDP, as a deficit of over about 5% of GDP is more worrying because it relies heavily on foreigners continuing to invest."],
  ["A developing economy is more vulnerable because investors are quicker to fear a downturn and withdraw their money The causeA large, persistent deficit may signal weak competitiveness and weak supply-side policy; or it may simply reflect high incomes or demographics How it is financedBorrowing to fund investment that raises productive capacity is healthy; borrowing to fund consumption is not", "A developing economy is more vulnerable because investors are quicker to fear a downturn and withdraw their money. The cause matters, as a large, persistent deficit may signal weak competitiveness and weak supply-side policy, or it may simply reflect high incomes or demographics. How it is financed also matters, as borrowing to fund investment that raises productive capacity is healthy, while borrowing to fund consumption is not."],
  ["Type of firmProfit or not-for-profitLong-term profit maximiser?", "Each type of firm is compared on whether it is profit or not-for-profit and whether it is a long-term profit maximiser."],
  ["Public limited company (private sector)ProfitUnlikely.", "A public limited company, which is in the private sector, aims for profit but is unlikely to be a long-term profit maximiser."],
  ["Profit SATISFICING is the most likely behaviour Privately owned firm (private sector)ProfitMost likely to genuinely profit maximise - the owner IS the manager, so there is no principal–agent gap Public sector organisationNot-for-profitNo.", "Profit satisficing is the most likely behaviour for a public limited company. A privately owned firm in the private sector aims for profit and is the most likely to genuinely profit maximise, because the owner is the manager, so there is no principal-agent gap. A public sector organisation is not-for-profit and so is not a long-term profit maximiser."],
  ["CriticismExplanation Firms rarely operate on the marginCalculating MC and MR precisely is difficult; firms do not adjust output one unit at a time, and ceteris paribus never holds in practice.", "Firms rarely operate on the margin, because calculating MC and MR precisely is difficult, firms do not adjust output one unit at a time, and ceteris paribus never holds in practice."],
  ["Cost-plus pricingMany firms simply add a percentage mark-up to average cost, which is a rule of thumb rather than an optimisation.", "Cost-plus pricing: many firms simply add a percentage mark-up to average cost, which is a rule of thumb rather than an optimisation."],
  ["SatisficingFirms may aim for a satisfactory profit that keeps stakeholders content, rather than the maximum.", "Satisficing: firms may aim for a satisfactory profit that keeps stakeholders content, rather than the maximum."],
  ["Long-run against short-runSacrificing profit now - through low prices, R&D or CSR - may raise long-run profit, so apparent deviations may be profit maximisation over a longer horizon.", "Long-run against short-run: sacrificing profit now, through low prices, R&D or CSR, may raise long-run profit, so apparent deviations may be profit maximisation over a longer horizon."],
  ["ObjectiveRoute to long-run profit Sales volume maximisationBuilds economies of scale and market power, letting the firm outcompete rivals and making its demand curve more inelastic later.", "Sales volume maximisation builds economies of scale and market power, letting the firm outcompete rivals and making its demand curve more inelastic later."],
  ["Sales revenue maximisationRaises the finance needed for capital investment, lowering costs or raising quality in the long run.", "Sales revenue maximisation raises the finance needed for capital investment, lowering costs or raising quality in the long run."],
  ["SurvivalStaying in business is a precondition for any future profit at all.", "Survival: staying in business is a precondition for any future profit at all."],
  ["Take Ford Motor Company as a single running example, so each type is unambiguous: TypeWhat Ford would buyAdvantagesDisadvantages HorizontalRenault - a direct competitorRemoves a competitor; greater economies of scale; larger market share and more pricing controlHostility and job losses; changes may damage customer loyalty; expensive to purchase Forward verticalA car showroom - later in the supply chainGuarantees an outlet for its products; cuts out the middleman, raising profit; more control over pricing and product displayEntering a new market can distract from core activities, since resources and expertise must be shared Backward verticalA rubber plantation for tyres - earlier in the supply chainGuarantees quality of inputs and security of supply; cuts out the middleman; can restrict supplies to competitorsSame distraction risk - running a plantation is not making cars ConglomerateLidl - an unrelated marketSpreads risk across different markets; targets new customers; gains assets, customers and expertise from the acquired firmMay lack the knowledge to run the new business successfully; distraction from the core market", "Take Ford Motor Company as a single running example, so each type is unambiguous. Horizontal integration would be buying Renault, a direct competitor, which removes a competitor, gives greater economies of scale and a larger market share and more pricing control, but causes hostility and job losses, changes may damage customer loyalty and it is expensive to purchase. Forward vertical integration would be buying a car showroom, later in the supply chain, which guarantees an outlet for its products, cuts out the middleman, raising profit, and gives more control over pricing and product display, but entering a new market can distract from core activities, since resources and expertise must be shared. Backward vertical integration would be buying a rubber plantation for tyres, earlier in the supply chain, which guarantees quality of inputs and security of supply, cuts out the middleman and can restrict supplies to competitors, but carries the same distraction risk because running a plantation is not making cars, while conglomerate integration would be buying Lidl, an unrelated market, which spreads risk across different markets, targets new customers and gains assets, customers and expertise from the acquired firm, but the firm may lack the knowledge to run the new business successfully and may be distracted from the core market."],
  ["EfficiencyAchieved?Why ProductiveNoThe firm deliberately restricts output below the minimum efficient scale to keep prices high AllocativeNoIt charges above marginal cost (P > MC), so too few resources go to the good DynamicUncertainTraditional theory says no: with no competition there is no pressure to invest in R&D.", "Productive efficiency is not achieved, because the firm deliberately restricts output below the minimum efficient scale to keep prices high. Allocative efficiency is not achieved, because it charges above marginal cost (P > MC), so too few resources go to the good. Dynamic efficiency is uncertain, because traditional theory says it is not achieved: with no competition there is no pressure to invest in R&D."],
  ["But the supernormal profit may fund innovation designed to keep barriers high and prevent future entry X-efficiencyNoWithout competitive pressure, organisational slack can develop Consumer welfare featurePresent?Why Low pricesNoMarket power and barriers let the firm restrict output and charge high prices without fear of entry High qualityUnlikelyConsumers have nowhere else to go, so there is little incentive to improve.", "But the supernormal profit may fund innovation designed to keep barriers high and prevent future entry. X-efficiency is not present, because without competitive pressure organisational slack can develop. Low prices are not present, because market power and barriers let the firm restrict output and charge high prices without fear of entry. High quality is unlikely, because consumers have nowhere else to go, so there is little incentive to improve."],
  ["Supernormal profit may still be spent on innovation to protect the barriers ChoiceNoThere is only one firm", "Supernormal profit may still be spent on innovation to protect the barriers. Choice is not present, because there is only one firm."],
  ["ReasonExplanation Size of the marketA niche or local market cannot support a large firm - a village bakery, a specialist repairer.", "One reason is the size of the market. A niche or local market cannot support a large firm, for example a village bakery or a specialist repairer."],
  ["Access to financeSmall firms struggle to raise capital, especially without collateral or a track record.", "Another reason is access to finance. Small firms struggle to raise capital, especially without collateral or a track record."],
  ["FlexibilitySmall firms respond faster to changing conditions and can offer personal service that large firms cannot.", "Another reason is flexibility. Small firms respond faster to changing conditions and can offer personal service that large firms cannot."],
  ["PolicyHow it worksLimitation Expenditure switching (depreciation, tariffs)Makes imports dear and exports cheap, switching spending towards domestic output.Requires the Marshall–Lerner condition; the J-curve delays it; it is inflationary; tariffs invite retaliation.", "Expenditure switching (depreciation, tariffs) makes imports dear and exports cheap, switching spending towards domestic output. Its limitations are that it requires the Marshall-Lerner condition, the J-curve delays it, it is inflationary, and tariffs invite retaliation."],
  ["Expenditure reducing (contractionary fiscal or monetary policy)Lowers domestic incomes, so import demand falls.Works, but at the cost of lower growth and higher unemployment - treating the symptom by shrinking the economy.", "Expenditure reducing (contractionary fiscal or monetary policy) lowers domestic incomes, so import demand falls. It works, but at the cost of lower growth and higher unemployment, treating the symptom by shrinking the economy."],
  ["Supply-side policyRaises productivity and quality, improving competitiveness at the root.The only lasting solution, but it works over years, not months.", "Supply-side policy raises productivity and quality, improving competitiveness at the root. It is the only lasting solution, but it works over years, not months."],
  ["If demand isRaising priceCutting price ElasticTotal revenue FALLS - quantity falls proportionally moreTotal revenue RISES InelasticTotal revenue RISES - quantity falls proportionally lessTotal revenue FALLS Unit elasticTotal revenue unchangedTotal revenue unchanged This links straight to tax policy: a government taxing for REVENUE should choose inelastic goods (tobacco, fuel); a government taxing to change BEHAVIOUR needs elastic demand or the consumption will not fall.", "If demand is elastic, raising price makes total revenue fall because quantity falls proportionally more, and cutting price makes it rise. If demand is inelastic, raising price makes total revenue rise because quantity falls proportionally less, and cutting price makes it fall, while with unit elastic demand total revenue is unchanged either way. This links straight to tax policy: a government taxing for REVENUE should choose inelastic goods (tobacco, fuel), while a government taxing to change BEHAVIOUR needs elastic demand or the consumption will not fall."],
  ["It depends onWhy The output gapWith a negative gap, stimulus raises output with little inflation.", "It depends on the output gap: with a negative gap, stimulus raises output with little inflation."],
  ["The size of the multiplierDetermined by the leakages MPS, MPT and MPM.", "It depends on the size of the multiplier, which is determined by the leakages MPS, MPT and MPM."],
  ["What the money is spent onCapital spending with a high social return shifts LRAS; current spending does not.", "It depends on what the money is spent on: capital spending with a high social return shifts LRAS, but current spending does not."],
  ["The starting debt positionHigh debt raises interest costs and constrains the room to act.", "It depends on the starting debt position: high debt raises interest costs and constrains the room to act."],
  ["It depends onWhy The CAUSE of the inflationThis is the decisive one.", "It depends on the CAUSE of the inflation, and this is the decisive one."],
  ["Where the economy is on ASWith spare capacity, loosening raises output; near capacity it raises only prices.", "It depends on where the economy is on AS: with spare capacity, loosening raises output, but near capacity it raises only prices."],
  ["CredibilityWith well-anchored expectations, small changes suffice.", "Credibility: with well-anchored expectations, small changes suffice."],
  ["AssumptionWhat it means A single sellerOne firm is the whole industry No substitutesThere are no firms selling similar products either, which gives the firm its market power High barriers to entry and exitThe firm can use its market power without fear of new entrants Short-run profit maximisationIt produces where MC = MR If an exam asks whether a firm shows monopoly behaviour, test it against these four assumptions in turn.", "The monopoly model assumes a single seller, so one firm is the whole industry, and no substitutes, so there are no firms selling similar products either, which gives the firm its market power. It also assumes high barriers to entry and exit, so the firm can use its market power without fear of new entrants, and short-run profit maximisation, so it produces where MC = MR. If an exam asks whether a firm shows monopoly behaviour, test it against these four assumptions in turn."],
  ["ConditionExplanation Low or no SUNK costsThe decisive one - costs recoverable on exit make \"hit and run\" entry possible Free entry and exitNo legal, technical or financial barriers Access to the same technologyEntrants can match incumbents’ costs Perfect informationPotential entrants can see the supernormal profit that makes entry worthwhile No consumer brand loyaltyCustomers will switch to an entrant Definition: Hit-and-run entryA firm enters to capture supernormal profit, then exits when the incumbent responds - profitable only if sunk costs are low.", "A contestable market needs low or no sunk costs, which is the decisive condition because costs recoverable on exit make \"hit and run\" entry possible. It also needs free entry and exit with no legal, technical or financial barriers, access to the same technology so entrants can match incumbents' costs, perfect information so potential entrants can see the supernormal profit that makes entry worthwhile, and no consumer brand loyalty so customers will switch to an entrant. Hit-and-run entry is when a firm enters to capture supernormal profit, then exits when the incumbent responds, and it is profitable only if sunk costs are low."],
  ["Consumers may LOSEConsumers may GAIN Higher prices from collusion; the outcome approaches monopolyPrice wars can make competition fierce and prices very low Allocative inefficiency where P > MCSupernormal profit funds R&D - high dynamic efficiency in pharmaceuticals, telecoms, aerospace Wasteful advertising raising costsEconomies of scale from large size can lower prices High barriers protect incumbents from entryNon-price competition raises quality and choice", "Consumers may lose from higher prices from collusion, where the outcome approaches monopoly, from allocative inefficiency where P > MC, from wasteful advertising raising costs, and from high barriers that protect incumbents from entry. Consumers may gain because price wars can make competition fierce and prices very low, and because supernormal profit funds R&D, giving high dynamic efficiency in pharmaceuticals, telecoms and aerospace. Economies of scale from large size can also lower prices, and non-price competition raises quality and choice."],
  ["More ELASTIC whenExplanation Labour is a large share of total costsA wage rise then has a large effect on total costs, so firms cut employment more Capital is a close substituteFirms can automate in response to higher wages Demand for the product is elasticFirms cannot pass higher wage costs on, so they cut employment instead The time period is longFirms have time to restructure and workers have time to retrain", "Labour demand is more elastic when labour is a large share of total costs, because a wage rise then has a large effect on total costs, so firms cut employment more. It is also more elastic when capital is a close substitute, because firms can automate in response to higher wages, or when demand for the product is elastic, because firms cannot pass higher wage costs on, so they cut employment instead. A long time period also makes it more elastic, because firms have time to restructure and workers have time to retrain."],
  ["FunctionExplanation Facilitate savingProvide secure vehicles for households and firms to store wealth Lend to businesses and individualsChannel savings into investment and consumption Allocate capitalDirect funds towards the most productive uses through the price mechanism Facilitate exchangeProvide payment systems and foreign exchange Provide a market for equitiesAllow firms to raise capital by issuing shares Forward markets and risk managementLet firms hedge against commodity price and exchange rate movements", "Financial markets facilitate saving by providing secure vehicles for households and firms to store wealth. They lend to businesses and individuals, channelling savings into investment and consumption, and allocate capital by directing funds towards the most productive uses through the price mechanism. They also facilitate exchange by providing payment systems and foreign exchange, provide a market for equities that allows firms to raise capital by issuing shares, and run forward markets that let firms hedge against commodity price and exchange rate movements and so manage risk."],
  ["StakeholderEffect of monopsony power The monopsonist firmLower input costs, higher profit; may pass some on as lower prices to consumers, or keep it Suppliers / workersLower prices or wages than their contribution justifies; squeezed margins; farmers driven out; workers underpaid ConsumersPossibly lower prices if cost savings are passed on - but not if the firm also has monopoly power in its selling market Employees of the monopsonistAmbiguous: a profitable employer may be more secure, but the same power may be used against its own workforce EfficiencyAllocatively inefficient: too little is bought at too low a price; the deadweight loss mirrors monopoly", "The monopsonist firm gains lower input costs and higher profit, and may pass some on as lower prices to consumers or keep it, while suppliers and workers get lower prices or wages than their contribution justifies, with squeezed margins, farmers driven out and workers underpaid. Consumers may get lower prices if cost savings are passed on, but not if the firm also has monopoly power in its selling market, and the monopsonist's own employees are affected ambiguously, because a profitable employer may be more secure but the same power may be used against its own workforce. On efficiency, it is allocatively inefficient, because too little is bought at too low a price and the deadweight loss mirrors monopoly."],
  ["ToolWhat it doesCost Cutting Bank RateLowers borrowing costs across the economyExhausted at the zero lower bound Quantitative easingBuys bonds to lower long-term yields and raise asset pricesInflates asset prices and so wealth inequality; hard to unwind Forward guidanceCommits to keeping rates low, shaping expectationsOnly works if believed; ties the Bank’s hands if conditions change Lender of last resortLends to solvent but illiquid banks to stop a runCreates moral hazard - banks expect rescue and take more risk Funding schemesCheap funding conditional on banks lending to the real economyBanks may use it to repair balance sheets instead", "Cutting Bank Rate lowers borrowing costs across the economy but is exhausted at the zero lower bound, and quantitative easing buys bonds to lower long-term yields and raise asset prices but inflates asset prices and so wealth inequality, and is hard to unwind. Forward guidance commits to keeping rates low, shaping expectations, but only works if believed and ties the Bank’s hands if conditions change. The lender of last resort lends to solvent but illiquid banks to stop a run, though it creates moral hazard, as banks expect rescue and take more risk, while funding schemes give cheap funding conditional on banks lending to the real economy, but banks may use it to repair balance sheets instead."],
  ["Nationalisation - arguments FORPrivatisation - arguments FOR Natural monopoly avoids wasteful duplication of networksProfit motive drives efficiency and cost reduction Externalities and social objectives can be pursued directlyRemoves political interference from investment decisions Avoids private monopoly exploiting consumersRaises revenue for government and widens share ownership Can guarantee universal service in unprofitable areasCompetition where it is possible improves choice and quality", "The arguments for nationalisation are that a natural monopoly avoids wasteful duplication of networks, externalities and social objectives can be pursued directly, it avoids private monopoly exploiting consumers, and it can guarantee universal service in unprofitable areas. The arguments for privatisation are that the profit motive drives efficiency and cost reduction, it removes political interference from investment decisions, it raises revenue for government and widens share ownership, and competition where it is possible improves choice and quality."],
])
